The note: "[2026-03-20] standup/atlas/2026-03-20: Priya runs the quarterly capacity review."

New durable fact: Priya runs the quarterly capacity review. Entities: Priya Raman (note says "Priya", existing entity name is "Priya Raman"). Should I add an alias for "Priya" → "Priya Raman"? The note uses "Priya" which likely refers to Priya Raman given the context (Atlas standup). Existing entity names include "Priya Raman". The instructions say: "When the note uses a different name for an existing entity, add an entity_aliases entry mapping the note's name to the existing name." So add alias "Priya" → "Priya Raman".

Fact content: "Priya Raman runs the quarterly capacity review." Entities: ["Priya Raman", "quarterly capacity review"]. Kind: "fact". Evidence: "Priya runs the quarterly capacity review." Confidence: high, maybe 0.9 or 1.0 — the note explicitly states it. I'll use 1 or 0.9. Let's say 0.9 (assuming "Priya" refers to Priya Raman). Actually the alias mapping is a reasonable inference given context. Use 0.9.

Relation: Priya Raman runs quarterly capacity review? The relations examples are leads, maintains, owns, depends_on, member_of. "runs" could be a rel_kind... The schema says "A short verb: leads, maintains, owns, depends_on, member_of." But "such as" implies examples. Existing fact "Yuki Tanaka runs the monthly cost review" was stored as a fact, not a relation. So keep consistent: just a fact, no relation.

New entity: "quarterly capacity review" — it's not in existing entity names, so the fact introduces it. That's fine.

Output JSON:

{
 "add_facts": [
  {
   "content": "Priya Raman runs the quarterly capacity review.",
   "entities": ["Priya Raman", "quarterly capacity review"],
   "kind": "fact",
   "confidence": 0.9,
   "evidence": "Priya runs the quarterly capacity review."
  }
 ],
 "entity_aliases": [
  {
   "name": "Priya",
   "canonical": "Priya Raman",
   "evidence": "Priya runs the quarterly capacity review."
  }
 ]
}

